{% extends 'help/article.html.twig' %}

{% block body %}
    <p>
        When you need a driver for a load, the app ranks your drivers against it and tells you
        <em>why</em> each one sits where they do. It never hides a driver behind a score you
        cannot interrogate — every candidate comes back with three lists.
    </p>

    <h2>Blockers, warnings and matches</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Blockers</strong> are hard stops. A hazmat load and a driver without the
            endorsement; freight heavier than their trailer's payload capacity; a driver already
            committed on those dates. A driver with a blocker is not eligible.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Warnings</strong> are things you may knowingly accept. The truck's last known
            position is a long way from the pickup, or the position is stale enough that the app
            will not vouch for it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Matches</strong> are the positive reasons. The right trailer type, temperature
            control for a reefer load, E-track for securement, close to the pickup.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="note">
        <p>Read the blockers before you override anything. "Not eligible" almost always traces back
        to a field nobody filled in — an endorsement left unchecked, or a trailer with no payload
        capacity — rather than to a driver who genuinely cannot take the work.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>What it checks</h2>
    <div class="help-scroll">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Checked</th><th>Against</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Hazmat and tanker endorsements</td><td>The load's hazmat flag and commodity</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Trailer type</td><td>The trailer type the load needs</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Payload capacity</td><td>The load's weight</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Temperature control</td><td>Whether the load sets a temperature</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Securement — E-track, straps, load bars</td><td>The load's securement requirements</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Distance from the pickup</td><td>The truck's last reported GPS position</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Date conflicts</td><td>Loads that driver is already on</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Stale positions</h2>
    <p>
        Proximity is only as good as the last position the ELD reported. If a truck has not
        reported recently, the app says so rather than quietly treating an old position as
        current — a truck that was near the pickup this morning may be three hundred miles away
        now. Treat a stale-position warning as "confirm with the driver", not as a reason to skip
        them.
    </p>

    <h2>Strict mode</h2>
    <p>
        By default you see everyone, ranked, blockers and all, so you can make the call. Strict
        mode hides anyone with a blocker. Use the default when you are short of drivers and need
        to see who is <em>nearly</em> right; use strict when you have plenty and only want clean
        options.
    </p>

    <h2>If nobody is suggested</h2>
    <p>Work through these in order — it is nearly always one of them:</p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Drivers have no assigned truck.</strong> A driver with no truck has no trailer and no position, so almost everything fails. Fix it on the <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'trucks-and-trailers' }) }}">truck record</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Trailer records are incomplete.</strong> No payload capacity means the weight check cannot pass.</li>
        <li><strong>Endorsements were never ticked.</strong> Common right after an ELD import — it brings licence numbers, not endorsement flags.</li>
        <li><strong>Driver status.</strong> Only active drivers are offered. Someone on leave or off duty will not appear.</li>
    </ol>
{% endblock %}
